From 9f25f0cf4185596aaaa21f4f6fc3c2d66d1eaabe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Minh Ha-Duong <minh.haduong@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 09:33:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(search): refuse mode:"semantic" when no provider can
 embed the query (#7)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Semantic ranking needs both ends of the comparison — vectors in the index, and a
provider to turn the query into one — but the refusal only tested the first.

reconcileVectorProvenance drops stored vectors only when a current embedder
identity disagrees with the stored one, so with ZOTEUS_EMBEDDINGS=off there is
nothing to disagree and the rows are kept: unusable, not known-wrong. hasVectors
therefore stays true, the 0-vector refusal never fires, and query() closes the
keyword ranker (mode !== 'semantic' is false) and builds no query vector
(opts.embedder is undefined), returning []. The tool renders that as a bare
`No matches for "…"`, indistinguishable from a library that holds nothing on the
subject — the #7 failure mode the refusal exists to prevent. Nothing else covers
it: embedderNotice is deliberately silent when a provider was switched off on
purpose, so the one sentence that would have explained the empty page is
suppressed by design.

The gate now also requires embedderId, and the choice of that flag over
hasEmbedder is the substance of this commit. embedderId is undefined exactly
when there is no provider object, which is what query() itself keys on, so it
marks the case where no query can ever be embedded. hasEmbedder is
embedderActive, which additionally goes false on the first query-time failure
and is sticky: noteEmbedFailure records only the first edge, and embedderError
is cleared solely by build/buildIncremental/updateIncremental, never by a later
success. query() ignores the flag and keeps using the provider, so after a 429
the next semantic query re-embeds and ranks normally. Refusing on hasEmbedder
would convert one rate-limit blip into a mode that stays dead until the user
rebuilds the index — while auto went on embedding the same query successfully.
A transient failure keeps being reported by embedderNotice on the query that
hit it, which is the existing contract.

The 0-vector wording is unchanged. The new branch says how many vectors are
stranded and either names the provider that built them (vectorEmbedderId
survives the switch-off) or names the configured provider and why it is not
active. It is the same refusal an index with no vectors and no provider already
got, which differed only in whether the index happened to hold rows the query
could not reach.

